1. What we used (The Tech Stack)
Spring Boot 3.x: For rapid application development with auto-configuration.

Spring Security: To implement Role-Based Access Control (RBAC).

MockMvc: To simulate HTTP requests (GET, POST, DELETE) without starting a real server,
allowing for faster testing of the web layer.

Lombok: Specifically @Data, @Builder, and @RequiredArgsConstructor to reduce boilerplate code in DTOs and Controllers.
Jackson (ObjectMapper): For serializing Java objects to JSON and vice-versa, specifically
handling complex @RequestPart multipart data.

2. What we did (The Testing Strategy)
Integration Testing: We loaded the ApplicationContext using @SpringBootTest to verify that our Security filters, Controllers, and Service mocks work together.
Service Mocking: We used @MockBean to isolate the Controller from the database. This allowed us to focus on testing API contracts
and security rules without needing real DB connections.
Multipart/Form-Data Handling: We successfully tested endpoints that accept both JSON data and physical files (like product images)
using MockMultipartFile.
Negative Testing: We didn't just test success; we verified that a USER receives a 403 Forbidden when attempting ADMIN actions.

