To explain the JwtRequestFilter in an interview, you should describe it as the "Security Guard" of your application. It sits in front of your Controllers and checks every request for a valid "ID card" (the JWT).
Based on the code in your PDF (Pages 16-17), here is the step-by-step flow you should explain:
1. The "Once Per Request" Rule
What to say: "I implemented JwtRequestFilter by extending OncePerRequestFilter. This ensures that the security logic only runs once for every HTTP request, preventing redundant processing." (pp. 16-17)
2. The Step-by-Step Security Flow
When an interviewer asks how it works, explain these 4 steps:
Extract the Header: The filter looks for the Authorization header. It specifically checks if it starts with the string "Bearer " followed by the token. (p. 17)
Validate the Token: Using my JwtUtil class, the filter extracts the Email (Username) from the token and checks if the token has expired. (pp. 17-18)
Load User Details: If the token is valid and the user is not yet authenticated in the current session, it calls AppUserDetailsService to fetch the user's data and Roles (like ADMIN or USER) from the database. (pp. 17, 20)
Set Security Context: Finally, it creates a UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken and stores it in the SecurityContextHolder. This tells Spring Security: "This user is verified; let them pass to the Controller." (p. 17)
3. Why is this important for a POS?
What to say: "In a POS system, security is critical. By using JWT, we keep the backend Stateless. We don't need to store sessions on the server, which makes the system faster and more scalable for multiple cashiers or stores." (p. 15)
Interview "Pro-Tip" Questions & Answers:
Q: What happens if the token is invalid?
A: "The filter simply fails to set the authentication in the SecurityContextHolder. Later, the SecurityFilterChain will see there is no authenticated user and return a 401 Unauthorized or 403 Forbidden based on the URL's requirements." (pp. 15, 17)
Q: Why do you check SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication() == null?
A: "This prevents re-authenticating a user who is already authenticated for the current request, which saves processing time." (p. 17)
